# Copy this file to .env and adjust values, then run: python -m freqscan
#
# Each backend runs if its section below is present. Fill in any combination of
# RTL__*, HACKRF__*, and PLUTO__* (or PLUTO_STARE__* instead of PLUTO__*, see below) to
# run those backends at the same time.

# ---- Shared ----
WATERFALL_ROWS=100
# plot_backend: which of plotting/{matplotlib,pyqtgraph}_backend.py renders the live
# window. "matplotlib" (default) needs no extra dependency. "pyqtgraph" redraws
# meaningfully faster once more than ~2 subplots are active, but needs the
# `plot-pyqtgraph` optional extra installed (pip install -e ".[plot-pyqtgraph]").
PLOT_BACKEND=matplotlib
# plot_refresh_ms: how often the whole plot redraws (one shared animation loop for
# every channel, so this is global, not per-backend, regardless of which plot_backend
# above is chosen). 200ms suits sweep-mode backends fine (a hop arrives far slower than
# that anyway); a PLUTO_STARE__* backend can produce hundreds of FFT windows/sec, so
# lower this (e.g. 50) to actually see more of that throughput instead of redrawing the
# same single latest window 5x/sec.
PLOT_REFRESH_MS=200

# ---- RTL-SDR settings (present => this backend runs) ----
RTL__GAIN=50
# RTL__GAIN=auto   # omit -g entirely and let the tuner auto-gain instead of a fixed value
RTL__INTERVAL=1
RTL__DEVICES=[{"id":0,"freq_start":"80M","freq_stop":"120M","bin_width":10000,"edge_trim":0.3},{"id":1,"freq_start":"420M","freq_stop":"470M","bin_width":10000,"edge_trim":0.3},{"id":2,"freq_start":"850M","freq_stop":"900M","bin_width":10000,"edge_trim":0.3}]

# ---- HackRF settings (present => this backend runs) ----
HACKRF__LNA_GAIN=32
HACKRF__VGA_GAIN=20
HACKRF__AMP_ENABLE=false
# bin_width: FFT bin width (frequency resolution) in Hz, passed to hackrf_sweep's single
# -w flag — shared by every range below (one physical device, one sweep process), unlike
# RTL__DEVICES where each device gets its own bin_width.
HACKRF__BIN_WIDTH=20000
HACKRF__RANGES=[{"freq_start":850,"freq_stop":950,"edge_trim":0.02},{"freq_start":2300,"freq_stop":2500,"edge_trim":0.05},{"freq_start":5700,"freq_stop":5900,"edge_trim":0.05}]

# ---- PlutoSDR-compatible settings (present => this backend runs) ----
# uri: libiio context URI — "ip:192.168.2.1" over USB's RNDIS/ECM network interface (the
# default), "usb:X.Y.Z" for a direct USB IIO connection, or "ip:pluto.local" if mDNS resolves.
PLUTO__URI=ip:192.168.2.1
# channels: one JSON object per AD9361 RX chain to capture — list has 1 entry (RX1 only)
# by default. AD9361 has no discrete on/off RX amplifier to enable/disable (checked
# against this project's own board schematic — SMAs connect to the chip through passive
# baluns only, no LNA/PA populated); gain_control_mode/hardwaregain per entry below is
# the actual controllable gain path, analogous in spirit to HACKRF__LNA_GAIN/VGA_GAIN
# above. Both fields are optional per entry and default to slow_attack/40.0 if omitted —
# only "channel" (1 or 2, which RX chain) is required.
#
# RX1 and RX2 physically share the same RX_LO/sample_rate/rf_bandwidth — there's one
# shared local oscillator and ADC clock feeding both antenna inputs on the AD9361 chip
# itself (true of every AD9361-based radio, not something this project's config could
# change), so PLUTO__BIN_WIDTH/CAPTURE_BANDWIDTH/RANGES below always apply to both
# channels identically. Only gain can differ per channel. Two full examples:
#
#   just RX1, default gain (equivalent to omitting this line entirely):
#   PLUTO__CHANNELS=[{"channel":1}]
#
#   both RX1 and RX2 (dual-antenna diversity monitoring, not independent frequencies —
#   see above), each with its own fixed manual gain instead of the slow_attack default:
#   PLUTO__CHANNELS=[{"channel":1,"gain_control_mode":"manual","hardwaregain":30.0},{"channel":2,"gain_control_mode":"manual","hardwaregain":50.0}]
PLUTO__CHANNELS=[{"channel":1}]
# bin_width: FFT bin width (frequency resolution) in Hz — shared by every range below,
# same one-process-multi-range model as HACKRF__BIN_WIDTH (Pluto is also a single
# exclusive-access physical radio, unlike RTL__DEVICES' per-device bin_width).
PLUTO__BIN_WIDTH=25000
# capture_bandwidth: per-hop instantaneous RX bandwidth in Hz (Pluto/AD9361 hardware max
# ~56MHz) — a wider value covers more spectrum per hop (fewer hops needed to sweep a
# given range) at the cost of fewer bins per Hz for the same bin_width above.
PLUTO__CAPTURE_BANDWIDTH=20000000
PLUTO__RANGES=[{"freq_start":100,"freq_stop":115,"edge_trim":0.1},{"freq_start":400,"freq_stop":420,"edge_trim":0.1}]

# ---- PlutoSDR "stare" mode: continuous single-frequency monitoring instead of sweeping
#      (present => this backend runs INSTEAD of PLUTO__* above — they're mutually
#      exclusive, both would fight over the same exclusive-access physical radio) ----
# Sweeping trades dwell time for coverage: with N hops, you only look at any one
# frequency ~1/N of the time. Stare mode is the other extreme — one fixed frequency,
# watched continuously (one long-lived capture, never retuned) — better for a brief or
# intermittent signal you'd otherwise miss between hops, better SNR via longer averaging
# at that one frequency, but zero visibility into the rest of the spectrum meanwhile.
# URI: usb: (direct USB bulk transfer) is meaningfully faster than ip:192.168.2.1
# (network-over-USB backend) — measured ~22MB/s vs ~16.5MB/s sustained for the same
# 2-channel capture — and PlutoStareBackend captures every configured channel from one
# shared connection specifically so usb: is usable at all with 2+ channels configured
# (its interface claim is exclusive: a second concurrent usb: connection to the same
# device fails outright). Use the bare "usb:" form (no bus.device.interface address) —
# libiio auto-resolves it to whichever single USB context is currently connected, so it
# survives reboots/reconnects that would otherwise change a specific address like
# "usb:1.82.5" to something else (confirmed live: a Pluto reconnect broke a hardcoded
# address this way). Only ambiguous with more than one PlutoSDR-compatible device
# plugged in at once — pass a specific "usb:x.y.z" (from `iio_info -s`) if that ever
# applies. ip: still works fine and is more portable if usb: isn't available.
# PLUTO_STARE__URI=usb:
# PLUTO_STARE__FREQUENCY=2437
# bin_width/sample_rate here mean the same thing as PLUTO__BIN_WIDTH/CAPTURE_BANDWIDTH,
# just for the one fixed window around FREQUENCY instead of a swept range. sample_rate
# is capped at Pluto's ~56MHz hardware max regardless (going wider needs sweep mode's
# multi-range hopping instead) -- bin_width is the knob that actually controls
# real-time throughput here. The real per-line cost is Python-side FFT + formatting CPU
# time, which scales with n_bins (sample_rate / bin_width) — not raw USB bandwidth,
# which sits far below its own ceiling across this whole practical range once every
# configured channel shares one connection (see PlutoStareBackend's docstring).
# Measured live: rate * n_bins stays roughly constant (~420,000) at sample_rate=50MHz —
# 10000 (n_bins=5000) sustains ~104 real lines/sec for 2 RX channels together, ~5x over
# the display's ~20Hz need; going much finer than this (e.g. 2500, n_bins=20000) lands
# right at ~20Hz with no safety margin left.
# PLUTO_STARE__BIN_WIDTH=10000
# PLUTO_STARE__SAMPLE_RATE=50000000
# PLUTO_STARE__EDGE_TRIM=0.1
# channels: same shape/meaning as PLUTO__CHANNELS above — one JSON object per AD9361 RX
# chain, gain_control_mode/hardwaregain optional per entry (default slow_attack/40.0),
# only "channel" (1 or 2) required. RX1/RX2 still share this section's FREQUENCY/
# SAMPLE_RATE (one shared RX_LO/ADC clock on the chip, see PLUTO__CHANNELS' note above) —
# only gain can differ. Two full examples:
#
#   just RX1, default gain (equivalent to omitting this line entirely):
#   PLUTO_STARE__CHANNELS=[{"channel":1}]
#
#   both RX1 and RX2 watching the same FREQUENCY (dual-antenna diversity, not independent
#   frequencies), each with its own fixed manual gain instead of the slow_attack default:
#   PLUTO_STARE__CHANNELS=[{"channel":1,"gain_control_mode":"manual","hardwaregain":30.0},{"channel":2,"gain_control_mode":"manual","hardwaregain":50.0}]
# PLUTO_STARE__CHANNELS=[{"channel":1}]

# ---- Kafka streaming (present => bins flagged above their adaptive noise floor are
#      published to this MSK cluster; omit this whole section to run purely local plotting) ----
# bootstrap_servers: from `terraform output bootstrap_brokers_public_sasl_iam` (terraform/)
# enabled: flip to false to keep the rest of this section saved but skip streaming
KAFKA__ENABLED=true
KAFKA__BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=broker1.example.com:9098,broker2.example.com:9098
KAFKA__TOPIC=freqscan.signals
# metadata_topic: one-time per-channel grid layout (freq range/resolution/bin count),
# published once at startup — lets python -m freqscan.kafka_viewer build a fixed,
# stable waterfall grid instead of discovering it from whatever bins get flagged over
# time. Must be provisioned with the same partition count as KAFKA__TOPIC.
KAFKA__METADATA_TOPIC=freqscan.signals.metadata
KAFKA__REGION=eu-central-1
# security_protocol/sasl_mechanism: real MSK always uses SASL/IAM — set both explicitly
# here for a local, non-MSK broker (e.g. smoke-testing the producer against a native
# Kafka install without touching AWS, or a machine on the same LAN connecting to one).
# Every machine's own .env needs this override independently, it isn't inherited from
# wherever the broker itself is running. For real MSK, comment both back out (or set
# security_protocol back to SASL_SSL) to use the SASL/IAM defaults.
KAFKA__SECURITY_PROTOCOL=PLAINTEXT
KAFKA__SASL_MECHANISM=
# For a self-hosted broker reachable over the public internet (e.g. a field Pi with no
# AWS credentials publishing to a broker on a cloud VM) — real username/password auth
# plus TLS, instead of either PLAINTEXT (no auth at all) or AWS IAM (which needs real
# AWS credentials the Pi doesn't have):
# KAFKA__SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_SSL
# KAFKA__SASL_MECHANISM=SCRAM-SHA-512
# KAFKA__SASL_USERNAME=pi2
# KAFKA__SASL_PASSWORD=<per-device secret, provisioned on the broker via kafka-configs.sh>
# ssl_ca_location: only needed when the broker's TLS cert is self-signed (as on a
# self-hosted broker, unlike real MSK's already-publicly-trusted cert) — path to that
# same cert, pinned here rather than trusted via a system CA bundle.
# KAFKA__SSL_CA_LOCATION=/home/dragon/PythonProjects/ew_freq_scanner/kafka-ca.pem
KAFKA__BASELINE_WINDOW=50
# signal_margin_db: dB above a bin's adaptive noise floor before it's flagged and published.
# Applies to any RTL device id / HackRF or Pluto range index not listed in the
# per-device/per-range override maps below (optional, commented out — uncomment and
# adjust to override specific devices/ranges, keyed by RTL__DEVICES' "id" and 0-based
# position in HACKRF__RANGES / PLUTO__RANGES).
KAFKA__SIGNAL_MARGIN_DB=8.0
# KAFKA__RTL_MARGINS_DB={"0":10.0,"1":8.0,"2":6.0}
# KAFKA__HACKRF_MARGINS_DB={"0":8.0,"1":6.0,"2":10.0}
# KAFKA__PLUTO_MARGINS_DB={"0":8.0,"1":6.0}
# spatial_margin_db: also require a bin to beat its own hop's median by this many dB
# before it's flagged, not just its own history — catches a whole band being uniformly
# noisy without treating that as N different signals. Same per-device/per-range
# override pattern as above.
KAFKA__SPATIAL_MARGIN_DB=8.0
# KAFKA__RTL_SPATIAL_MARGINS_DB={"0":10.0,"1":8.0,"2":6.0}
# KAFKA__HACKRF_SPATIAL_MARGINS_DB={"0":8.0,"1":6.0,"2":10.0}
# KAFKA__PLUTO_SPATIAL_MARGINS_DB={"0":8.0,"1":6.0}
# prominence_margin_db: flags a bin regardless of its own history if it's simply far
# enough above its own hop's median right now — an OR alongside the temporal check
# above, not a refinement of it like spatial_margin_db. For signals that are always
# there and always strong (e.g. a steady FM broadcast carrier): the temporal check
# alone never flags these, since the adaptive baseline just learns to expect them. Use
# a generous margin (bigger than spatial_margin_db) so this doesn't also catch
# generically noisy broadband bins.
KAFKA__PROMINENCE_MARGIN_DB=20.0
# Per-device/per-range override (optional) — e.g. this RTL device needed a smaller
# margin than the 20.0 default above to reliably catch a real, steady, but comparatively
# less prominent signal (see README's "How it works" for how to measure this yourself).
KAFKA__RTL_PROMINENCE_MARGINS_DB={"0":12.0}
# KAFKA__HACKRF_PROMINENCE_MARGINS_DB={"0":20.0,"1":20.0,"2":20.0}
# KAFKA__PLUTO_PROMINENCE_MARGINS_DB={"0":20.0,"1":20.0}
